title: Polycythaemia Vera
---

# Polycythaemia Vera

- **Definition** - form of MPN characterised by excessive proliferation of marrow precursor cells resulting in trillineage expansion, characteristically presenting w/ polycythaemia and related Sx
- **Epidemiology** - typically presents \> 40y
- **Clinical features of PRV:**
  - **Manifestations of hyperviscosity:**
    - <u>Non-specific Sx</u> - lassitude, loss of concentration, headaches, dizziness, blackouts
    - <u>Manifestation of arterial thrombosis</u> - Sx of peripheral arterial disease or ischaemic stroke/ TIA, amourosis fugax
    - <u>Manifestation of venous thromboembolism</u> - e.g. unilateral leg pain and leg swelling
  - **Manifestations of abnormal haemostasis** - bleeding (especially bleeding peptic ulcers and epistaxis)
  - **Sx specific of PV:**
    - <u>Aquagenic pruritis</u> - unbearable pruritis following warm bath or shower
    - <u>Erythromelagia</u> - burning pain accompanied by colour changes in the presence of palpable pulses
  - **Peptic ulcer** - common
- **Signs of PRV:**
  - <u>Facial plethora</u> - ruddy cyanosis, with plethoric skin but central cyanosis due to increased concentration of deoxy-Hb
  - <u>Splenomegaly</u> - may be present at Dx
- **Ix:**
  - **CBC** - reflects trilineage expansion:
    - <u>Hb, RBC, HCT</u> - polycythaemia demonstrated by high HCT and Hb concentration
    - <u>WBC and differentials</u> - neutrophilic leucocytosis
    - <u>PLT</u> - thrombocytosis present
  - **BM examination:**
    - Presence of JAK2 mutation in 90% cases (diagnostic, but in -ve cases, diagnosis can be made by r/o of other causes)
    - Abnormal karyotype in marrow
    - Autonomous growth of in vitro culture of marrow to show growth-factor independence
- **Dx** - WHO diagnostic criteria of polycythaemia vera: 
![](../img/dx_criteria_of_pv.png)
- **Risk stratification** - risk stratification based on risk of <u>thrombotic events</u> (leading cause of preventable death in PV):
  - <u>Low-risk</u> - age \<= 60y AND no Hx of thrombosis
  - <u>High-risk</u> - age \> 60y OR Hx of thrombosis
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - **Goals of Mx:**
      - <u>Control RBC mass</u> - targeting **HCT \< 45% and absence of Sx** by therapeutic phlebectomy +/- cytoreductive therapy
      - <u>Prevent thrombotic events</u> - by aspirin +/- cytoreductive therapy
      - <u>Control of cardiovascular RF</u> - reduce cardiovascular risk factors
    - **Approach to Mx** - dependent on risk of patients:
      - <u>Low-risk PV</u> - low-dose ASA + periodic phlbectomy
      - <u>High-risk PV</u> - low-dose ASA + cytoreductive therapy (w/ therapeutic phlembectomy initially until HCT adequately controlled by cytoreductive therapy)

      
      ![](../img/tx_algo_for_pv.png)
  - **Venesection** - prompt relief of hyperviscosity Sx:
    - <u>Principles</u>:
      - 400-500 mL of blood removed each time
      - Repeated every 5-7d until HCT normalised
      - Less frequent venesection to maintain this level until iron-deficiency ensues to keep Hb low
  - **Low-dose aspirin therapy** (40-100 mg/d):
    - <u>Indications</u> - for all PV patients
    - <u>MOA</u> - antiplatelet therapy
    - <u>Clinical efficacy</u>:
      - Low dose a/w reduction in risk of combined endpoint of non-fatal myocardial infarction, nonfatal stroke, pulmonary embolism and major venous thrombosis (RR 0.40 \[95% CI 0.18-0.91\])
    - <u>C/I for low-dose aspirin in MPN patients</u> - acquired von Willebrand syndrome
    - <u>S/E</u>:
      - GI bleeding
  - **Cytoreductive therapy:**
    - **Hydroxyurea:**
      - <u>Indications</u> - typically first-line in patients w/ no potential for pregnancy due to favourable S/E profile
      - <u>MOA</u> - inhibits DNA synthesis by inhibition of ribonucleotide reductase
      - <u>Dosing and regimen</u> - 500 mg bid:
        - Dose adjustment by weight, i.e. 15-20 mg bid
        - Half-dose if eGFR \< 60 ml/min
      - <u>S/E</u>:
        - Mucositis (mucocutaneous ulcers, diarrhoea)
        - Alopecia
        - Marrow suppression (cytopenias)
        - Peripheral neuropathy
      - <u>Clinical efficacy</u>:
        - Improved Sx and fewer thrombotic events
        - Improved survival
        - Complete response in 50% patients
      - <u>C/I</u> (generally avoided in all women w/ pregnancy potential):
        - Pregnancy
        - Breast-feeding
    - **Pegylated INF-alpha:**
      - <u>Indications</u> - pregnant women
      - <u>MOA</u>:
        - Suppression of megakaryopoeisis
        - Inhibition of proliferation of human Erythroid colonizing forming cells
        - Decreases in human bone marrow fibroblasts
      - <u>Dosing and regimen</u> - SC administration:
        - 100 mcg every 2 weeks
        - Dose increased by 50 mcg every 2 weeks until normalisation of haematological parameters
        - Maximum dose of 500 mcg every 2 weeks
      - <u>S/E</u>:
        - Flu-like syndrome (myalgia and arthralgia)
        - Injection site reactions
        - Hepatotoxicity
        - Depression
- **Prognosis** - median OS \> 10y, however major morbidities include:
  - Thrombotic events (60% experience CAD and CVA)
  - Transformation into leukaemia and MF (25%)
